*.s47
# *.zip is upstream's own rule and stays unqualified ON PURPOSE. Every .zip
# upstream tracks is a DERIVED archive its Makefile builds (`zip -r ... ALLPGMS`
# for res/testPgms/testPgms.zip), and z47's own dist pipeline regenerates that one
# from source (build/dist.zig). So there is nothing here worth carrying, and a
# blanket `!/upstream/**/*.zip` exception is actively harmful: a stale local
# regeneration then becomes stageable and `git add -A` commits a build output that
# does not even match upstream's copy. The appnote source bundles were the only
# real exception and left with that directory. If a resync ever brings a .zip that
# is genuine SOURCE, add that path specifically -- not the glob.
*.zip
!xfnToC47user.s47
!xfnToR47user.s47
20*.REGS.TSV
20*.bmp
c47plotTest*.bmp
tags
/PROGRAMS
/SAVFILES
# The same two runtime output dirs, at the imported root: the host simulator and
# the testSuite now run with UPSTREAM_ROOT as their CWD (res/ is opened
# CWD-relative), so this is where they get created. Anchored, not bare -- a bare
# `PROGRAMS` would also match the TRACKED upstream/res/PROGRAMS and quietly make
# its files unaddable.
/upstream/PROGRAMS
/upstream/SAVFILES
# src/generated holds BOTH build artefacts and tracked sources, so name the
# artefacts rather than the directory. Upstream ignores the whole directory and
# gets away with it because git lets an ALREADY-tracked file override an ignore --
# but that only protects files already in the index. Importing a file the pin
# tracks but z47 does not yet have (constantsVerification.txt) hit the directory
# rule and was silently skipped by `git add -A`, which is how the imported tree
# ended up not matching its own pin.
/upstream/src/generated/constantPointers.c
/upstream/src/generated/constantPointers.h
/upstream/src/generated/constantPointers2.c
/upstream/src/generated/rasterFontsData.c
/upstream/src/generated/softmenuCatalogs.h
AGENTS.md

# Over_score testing agent coding
AGENTS.md

